# Brazilian Grocery Advertised Prices (sample)

## What it is

A sample of online grocery prices collected from Brazilian supermarket chains by
[{{name}}]({{site_url}}). Each row is one observation of one product in one store at one
moment. The export is generated by `scripts/amostra/exportar.py` from the production database,
read-only.

Three tables, as CSV and, when generated with pyarrow, as Parquet:

- `products`: canonical products (GTIN when known, name, brand, category, package size).
- `stores`: store units (chain, unit, city, state, country).
- `prices`: price observations (one row per product, store and capture).

CSV files write money with two decimal places; the Parquet files hold the same values as
float64.

## Coverage

- Country: Brazil (`country = BR`). Currency: BRL (`currency = BRL`).
- The service behind this sample covers {{facts.stores}} store units in {{facts.states}} states
  (as of {{facts.as_of}}).
- Default cut: the state of São Paulo (`state = SP`) and a three-month window
  (`--state SP --months 3`). The window is rolling: it ends on the export date, not on a month
  boundary. Other states and windows are produced by the same script.
- The published sample keeps only products with a verified GTIN: rows whose `products.gtin` is
  empty are dropped. The barcode is the only product key this sample relies on.
- Excluded by the export: prices copied from another store (`is_replicated = 1`).
- Never exported (retailer content): `image_url` and `description`.
- Price history starts in March 2026.

## Columns

`products.csv` / `products.parquet`

| Column | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `product_id` | integer | Canonical product identifier (stable within an export). |
| `gtin` | string | GTIN/EAN barcode, verified; never empty in the published sample. |
| `name` | string | Normalized product name. |
| `brand` | string | Normalized brand. |
| `category` | string | Canonical category name. |
| `size` | string | Package quantity (e.g. `500`), or free text such as `500 g` when not structured. |
| `unit` | string | Package unit as sold (e.g. `g`, `ml`, `un`). |

`stores.csv` / `stores.parquet`

| Column | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `store_id` | integer | Store unit identifier (stable within an export). |
| `chain` | string | Supermarket chain name. |
| `unit` | string | Store unit label as configured by the collector (city or label such as "Online"). |
| `city` | string | City. |
| `state` | string | Brazilian state code (UF). |
| `country` | string | ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 (`BR`). |

`prices.csv` / `prices.parquet`

| Column | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `product_id` | integer | Joins to `products.product_id`. |
| `store_id` | integer | Joins to `stores.store_id`. |
| `price` | decimal (2 places) | Advertised online price charged at capture, in BRL. |
| `price_regular` | decimal (2 places) | Regular ("de") price, in BRL; empty when not shown. |
| `is_promotion` | boolean | `true` when the store showed the price as an offer. |
| `unit_price` | decimal (2 places) | Price per normalized base unit (R$/kg, R$/L or R$/un); empty when the package size is unknown. |
| `currency` | string | ISO 4217 code (`BRL`). |
| `captured_at` | string | ISO 8601 timestamp in UTC, e.g. `2026-03-15T13:00:00Z`. |
| `source_url` | string | Product page URL at the store, as seen at capture time. |

## Freshness

Collection is weekly. Each product is sampled at a random minute of the week and then collected
in every store that sells it. `captured_at` is often older than 7 days: on {{facts.fresh_as_of}}, {{facts.fresh_7d_pct}}% of current prices were less than 7 days old and {{facts.fresh_30d_pct}}% less than 30 days. Products
collected in different weeks have different ages. The export is generated on request, so the
newest `captured_at` in a file shows how current it is.

## Honest limits

- These are advertised online prices, not shelf prices. They can differ from the price in a
  physical store, and from the price at checkout (coupons, loyalty prices, delivery fees and
  stock changes are not captured).
- In the full database, product identity is approximate when the GTIN is missing, because
  matching falls back to name and brand. This sample keeps only products with a GTIN, so
  matching inside it is done by barcode; different stores can still sell the same barcode under
  different names.
- `unit` in `stores` is not unique across cities for some chains. Do not use it alone as a key;
  use `store_id`.
- The history is short (from March 2026), so it is not suitable for long-term inflation
  statistics.
- Source timestamps were recorded as local time in UTC-3 without a time zone database. The
  export reads them as -03:00 and writes them in UTC with a `Z` suffix.
- Coverage is partial: it is the set of chains and units the collector can read, not the full
  market.
- The sample is a snapshot. It is not updated continuously; a new file is exported on request.

